const faq=[{"q": "What should I do with excess business cash?", "a": "Decide what the cash is for before you decide where it goes. Cash the business needs stays in the business. Cash it does not need can start building your investment business. What changes the answerHow steady your revenue is, what you owe, what you plan to buy or hire for, and what your tax bill looks like this year. How we approach itWe sort it by when you need it. Today covers the next year. Tomorrow covers the next few years. Whatever is left can go to Beyond, where it has time to grow."}, {"q": "How much cash should I keep in my business?", "a": "Enough to run the business through a bad stretch and take an opportunity when one shows up. For most owners that is a number of months of expenses, and the right number is different for every business. What changes the answerSeasonality, how fast customers pay you, your debt, and how much risk you are comfortable carrying. How we approach itWe help you set a number with your tax strategist. Once cash goes past that number, that is your prompt to put the extra to work."}, {"q": "How do I build wealth outside my business?", "a": "Pay yourself on purpose and give that money a job. Your business gives you control over your income. Your investment business gives you control over your future. What changes the answerHow much the business still needs to grow, and how much of what you own is tied to it today. How we approach itWe help you set a steady amount that moves out of the business and into investments organized by when you need them. A future sale then adds to what you have built. It does not have to carry everything."}, {"q": "How much of my net worth should be tied to my business?", "a": "There is no right percentage. The better question is what happens to your life if the business has a hard three years. What changes the answerYour age, your other assets, how sellable the business is, and what your family depends on. How we approach itWe lay out everything you own in one picture, then build the outside pieces so your life does not depend on one outcome."}, {"q": "What should I do before selling my business?", "a": "Start planning before you have a signed deal. Most of the tax and structure choices get harder or disappear once the paperwork is done. What changes the answerHow the deal is structured, how the business is owned, your state, and what you want the money to do afterward. How we approach itWe bring your tax strategist and attorney into the same room, set a clear target for what the sale needs to provide, and build the plan for the money before it arrives."}, {"q": "What should I do with the money after selling my business?", "a": "Slow down. Put it somewhere safe and easy to get to while you build the plan. The cost of waiting a few months is small. The cost of a rushed decision can be permanent. What changes the answerTaxes owed on the sale, the income you need now, and what you want this money to do for your family and beyond. How we approach itWe set aside what you need for taxes and the next year first. Then we build Tomorrow and Beyond in stages, so every dollar has a job before it moves."}, {"q": "What does a financial advisor do for college and professional athletes?", "a": "We help you connect spending, taxes, and investing into one plan, so the money from your sport keeps working after you stop playing. What changes the answerWhere you are in your career, how you get paid, and who is already on your team. How we approach itWe work alongside your agent, attorney, and tax strategist. We explain your options in plain language and you make the decisions."}, {"q": "Is NIL income taxable?", "a": "Yes, in general. That includes products or services you receive as payment. What changes the answerHow you are paid, whether taxes are withheld, the state you live and play in, and your expenses. How we approach itWe help you keep records and set money aside with your tax strategist all year, so there is no surprise in April."}, {"q": "How much of my NIL or endorsement income should I set aside for taxes?", "a": "There is no single percentage that fits every athlete. It depends on your total income, what is already withheld, and your state. What changes the answerA new deal, a move to a new state, or a jump in income. How we approach itWe set a number with your tax strategist, move it into its own account when each payment lands, and review it when your income changes."}, {"q": "Can RIPL help me say no when friends or family ask for money?", "a": "Yes. We help you set a giving plan with clear boundaries. If you want, you can send requests to us. What changes the answerWho you want to help, how much, and how often. How we approach itYou decide the rules and what can be shared. We can be the ones who say a request falls outside the plan. Your account details stay private."}, {"q": "Should I start a business with my sports income?", "a": "As you build margin, you will likely be drawn toward business opportunities. We believe the first business you build should be your investment business. What changes the answerHow much you have set aside already, how long the opportunity ties up your money, and who is running it. How we approach itWe build your investment business first. Once it is paying you, it can help fund the others. We also review each opportunity with your attorney and agent before you commit."}, {"q": "Do I need a big professional contract before talking with RIPL?", "a": "No. A first NIL deal can come before a first job. New income of any size brings decisions worth getting right early. What changes the answerWhether you are in high school, college, or the pros, and whether your parents are involved. How we approach itFor high school and college athletes we sit down with you and your parents together. We explain the services and the cost before you decide anything."}, {"q": "Do I have enough money to retire?", "a": "It depends on one number most people have never set: the income you want each year. Once you know that, the question has an answer. What changes the answerYour spending, other income such as Social Security or a pension, taxes, and how long the money needs to last. How we approach itWe set a clear target with you first. Then we look at what you have against that target and show you the gap or the cushion."}, {"q": "How much can I spend in retirement?", "a": "An amount your investments can support through good and bad markets. That is a range, and it should be reviewed every year. What changes the answerHow your money is invested, your tax picture, your other income, and how flexible your spending is. How we approach itWe organize your money by when you will spend it. The next year is ready to spend. The next few years are set up to be dependable. The rest keeps growing."}, {"q": "How much cash should I keep in retirement?", "a": "Enough that you never have to sell long-term investments at a bad time to pay this year's bills. What changes the answerYour yearly spending, your other income, and how you feel when markets drop. How we approach itThat is the job of the Today ring. It holds the money you may need in the next year and it stays easy to get to."}, {"q": "Which accounts should I withdraw from first?", "a": "The order matters because each account type is taxed differently. The right order is the one that leaves you with more after taxes over your whole retirement, which is not always the lowest tax bill this year. What changes the answerYour tax bracket now and later, required distributions, and what you want to leave to family or causes. How we approach itWe map it out year by year with your tax strategist and adjust as tax law and your life change."}, {"q": "When should I take Social Security?", "a": "Waiting increases your monthly benefit. Taking it early gives you more years of payments. Which is better depends on your health, your other income, and your spouse. What changes the answerWhether you are still working, your spouse's benefit, and whether you need the income now. How we approach itWe look at it as one piece of your income plan, next to your investments and your taxes."}, {"q": "What if the market drops right after I retire?", "a": "A drop early in retirement hurts most when you have to sell investments to pay your bills. The fix is to not be forced to sell. What changes the answerHow much you have set aside for the next several years and how flexible your spending is. How we approach itRisk is not volatility. Risk is your money not being ready when you are. Today and Tomorrow are built so near-term spending does not depend on what the market did this year."}, {"q": "What should I do with a large amount of cash?", "a": "Give it a job. Cash with no job tends to sit for years, and that has a cost too. What changes the answerWhen you will need the money, what it is for, and what you already own. How we approach itWe sort it into Today, Tomorrow, and Beyond. Some stays ready. Some is set up for the next few years. The rest goes to work for the long term."}, {"q": "Do I have too much cash?", "a": "Possibly, if cash has grown beyond your spending needs, upcoming commitments and a reserve that fits your life. The first step is deciding what that cash is there to do. What changes the answerA purchase coming up, uneven income, or a business that needs a cushion. How we approach itWe help you set a cash number. When your balance grows past it, that is your prompt to invest the extra."}, {"q": "How do I know if my portfolio has a strategy?", "a": "Ask what job each account is doing. If the answer is the same for every dollar, or there is no answer, you have positions. You do not have a strategy. What changes the answerNothing. This applies at any size.What changes the answerYour goals, timelines and how each holding contributes to the overall plan. How we approach itEvery dollar gets assigned a job, organized by when it is needed. Some dollars keep you safe. Some create income. Some grow. Some are for giving. Some are for the next generation."}, {"q": "What does risk really mean?", "a": "Risk is not volatility. Risk is your money not being ready when you are. What changes the answerYour timeline. Money you need next year and money you will not touch for twenty years face very different risks. How we approach itWe start with when you need the money and build from there. A risk score is not where we start."}, {"q": "What is a structured note?", "a": "A structured note is an investment issued by a bank where the terms are set up front: how it can gain, how it can lose, and when it comes back. What changes the answerThe bank that issues it, the length, what it is tied to, and the specific terms. No two are the same. How we approach itIt is one tool we may use for the Tomorrow ring, when knowing the terms ahead of time matters. We walk through every term with you before anything is bought."}, {"q": "Can I lose money in a structured note?", "a": "Yes. A note can lose value if the investment it is tied to falls past the level set in its terms, or if the bank that issued it fails. Selling before the end date can also mean getting back less. What changes the answerThe protection level in the terms, the strength of the issuing bank, and whether you hold it to the end. How we approach itWe only consider a note for money that can stay put for the full term, and we compare how you can gain, how you can lose, what you give up, and when you can access your money."}, {"q": "Are structured notes worth it?", "a": "For some money, yes. For other money, no. A note fits when its terms match the job that money has. It is the wrong tool for money you may need on short notice. What changes the answerYour timeline, your other investments, and the cost and terms of the specific note. How we approach itThe plan comes first and the tool comes second. If a simpler investment does the same job, we use the simpler one."}, {"q": "Am I paying more in taxes than I need to?", "a": "Possibly. It usually happens when your investments, your business, and your tax return are each handled by someone different and nobody looks at them together. What changes the answerHow you earn your income, where your investments are held, and how much you give. How we approach itWe do not make a decision just because it saves taxes. We want to know whether it leaves you better off after the taxes, the opportunity, and the tradeoffs."}, {"q": "How can I reduce taxes on my investments?", "a": "Three things do most of the work: which type of account holds each investment, when you sell, and how you give. What changes the answerYour bracket, your state, how long you have held each investment, and your goals for the money. How we approach itWe look at your accounts as one picture with your tax strategist and plan sales and giving across the year instead of in December."}, {"q": "Are my taxes and investments working together?", "a": "Ask whether investment decisions account for your tax picture, and whether your advisor and tax professional coordinate when needed. Separate accounts can still work together; disconnected decisions are the concern. What changes the answerHow complicated your income is and how many accounts you have. How we approach itWe bring your tax strategist into the conversation. Investment decisions and tax decisions get made in the same room."}, {"q": "What is QSBS and could it reduce taxes when I sell my business?", "a": "QSBS stands for qualified small business stock. If your company and your shares meet the rules, part or all of the gain on a sale can be excluded from federal tax. What changes the answerHow the company is structured, when and how you got your shares, how long you have held them, and your state. How we approach itThis one has strict rules, so we raise it early and work it through with your tax strategist and attorney well before a sale."}, {"q": "Should I donate appreciated stock instead of cash?", "a": "Often worth considering. A direct gift of eligible appreciated stock held for more than a year to a qualifying charity can avoid a sale by you and may qualify for a deduction. Holding periods, charity type and deduction limits matter. What changes the answerHow long you have held it, how much it has grown, and how much you give each year. How we approach itWe look at your giving plan and your portfolio together and pick what to give, so your giving does more and your portfolio stays in balance."}, {"q": "Should I convert to a Roth?", "a": "It can make sense when you expect to be in a higher tax bracket later than you are now, and you can pay the tax on the conversion from other money. What changes the answerYour income this year, your future income, and what you plan to leave to family. How we approach itWe run it year by year with your tax strategist. It is rarely all or nothing."}, {"q": "I have too much company stock. What should I do?", "a": "Start by deciding how much of your future you want riding on one company. Then build a plan to get there over time. What changes the answerTaxes on the gain, any rules on when you can sell, and how you feel about the company. How we approach itWe set a target amount to keep, then plan the rest around taxes, timing, and where that money goes next."}, {"q": "Should I sell my company stock?", "a": "You do not have to choose between all or none. Most people keep some and move some. What changes the answerYour gain, your tax bracket, what else you own, and what the money is for. How we approach itWe tie each sale to a job. Money you need in the next few years should not depend on one stock."}, {"q": "How much of my portfolio should be in one stock?", "a": "There is no single right number. Ask what happens to your plans if that stock drops by half. What changes the answerYour other assets, your income, and whether your paycheck depends on the same company. How we approach itWe look at your whole picture. If your income and your investments both depend on one company, that matters more than any percentage."}, {"q": "Should I diversify all at once or over time?", "a": "Over time usually spreads out the taxes and the timing. All at once removes the risk sooner. Either can be right. What changes the answerThe size of the gain, your tax picture this year and next, and how much of your net worth is involved. How we approach itWe lay out both paths with real numbers and your tax strategist, and you choose."}, {"q": "What is NUA and when should I consider it?", "a": "NUA stands for net unrealized appreciation. It is a tax rule that can lower the tax on company stock held inside a retirement plan when you leave the company. What changes the answerHow much the stock has grown inside the plan, your age, and how you take the rest of the plan. How we approach itThe rules are strict and the choice usually cannot be undone, so we work through it with your tax strategist before anything moves."}, {"q": "What should I do with a large inheritance?", "a": "Nothing fast. Put it somewhere safe while you sort out the taxes and decide what you want it to do. What changes the answerWhat kind of accounts you inherited, the rules on each, and your own goals. How we approach itWe sort out the rules on each account first, then give every dollar a job: Today, Tomorrow, or Beyond."}, {"q": "What financial decisions should I make during or after a divorce?", "a": "Get a clear picture of what you have, what you owe, and what you need to live on before you make any big moves. What changes the answerHow assets are divided, taxes on each one, and your income going forward. How we approach itWe work alongside your attorney, build a new picture of your money, and set a target that fits your life now."}, {"q": "What financial decisions should I make after my spouse dies?", "a": "Very few of them need to happen right away. Handle what has a deadline and give yourself time on the rest. What changes the answerAccount titles, beneficiaries, benefits, and your income needs. How we approach itWe make a short list of what is urgent, take care of it with you, and hold the larger decisions until you are ready."}, {"q": "I just came into a lot of money. What should I do first?", "a": "Pause. Set aside what you will owe in taxes, keep the rest safe and easy to reach, and do not commit to anything for a set period of time. What changes the answerWhere the money came from, the taxes on it, and who else is advising you. How we approach itWe bring everyone advising you into the same room, map out more than one path, and build in stages so you are making deliberate choices."}, {"q": "Do I really need this insurance policy?", "a": "It depends on what the policy is protecting and whether that need still exists. What changes the answerWho depends on you, what you owe, what the policy costs, and what you would give up by dropping it. How we approach itWe look at what the policy does, what it costs, and whether something else does the same job better."}];
